# What this is: the release-integrity lab -- scripts/install.sh driven against a release
# this repository built itself, inside a container that has no route to the network.
#
# Reproduce with:  scripts/install-integrity-in-docker.sh
#                  (needs docker only; building chaos-install-lab:local is the single
#                  step that touches the network, and the lab image is Debian bookworm
#                  slim plus curl, python3 and python3-cryptography)
#
# Host: Ubuntu 22.04 x86_64, Docker 29.8.1, python3-cryptography 38.0.4 inside the
# container (the distro build, i.e. what a Debian user would have).
# Commit under test: 60e215fe plus the working-tree change to scripts/install.sh this
# lab's own html-sums check called for.
#
# The first run of this lab was 33/34: `html-sums` refused the release but the log did
# not say why, and investigating that is what found the defect now fixed in
# download_github -- only the last candidate's reason was reported, so a mirror that
# answers 200 with an HTML error page was blamed on whichever public mirror failed last.
# The run below is after that fix.
#

== the machine doing the installing
   image:      chaos-install-lab:local
   installer:  /home/chaos/hunt/dev/projects/chaos-code/scripts/install.sh (working tree)
   release:    a fixture built inside the container, version 9.9.9 (never published)
   network:    --network none
   ok  github.com is unreachable from this container (curl exit 6, could not resolve host)
   ok  loopback is up, which is all the fake release endpoint needs
   ok  no prior install to inherit: no ~/.chaos, no chaos on PATH, no cargo
   ok  fixture built for this host's asset: chaos-linux-x64
fixture signature verifies
   ok  the fixture's own signature verifies against its own key before any installer runs
   ok  three distinct keys are in play: the installer's built-in one, the fixture's, and a second valid one
   ok  the fake release endpoint is listening on 127.0.0.1:8099

== the path a user actually takes
   ok  install from the fixture completed (exit 0)
   ok  checksum OK, against SHA256SUMS served by the fixture
   ok  signature OK, over the artifact bytes, under the key passed in the environment
   ok  what landed on disk runs and reports 9.9.9
   ok  bin/chaos and bin/agent are relative symlinks to downloads/chaos-9.9.9-linux-x86_64, with chaos-latest beside it
   ok  the fixture was the first candidate tried and no public mirror or origin was ever attempted
only chaos-linux-x64 , SHA256SUMS and the sidecar
   ok  the only files the fixture ever served were the artifact, SHA256SUMS and the sidecar
   ok  a second run is a no-op instead of a second download

== what a wrong byte does
   ok  tampered: refused (exit 1) -- error: checksum mismatch for chaos-linux-x64
   ok  tampered: nothing installed behind the refusal

== what a recomputed SHA256SUMS does
   ok  forged-sums: refused (exit 1) -- error: signature verification FAILED for chaos-linux-x64
   ok  forged-sums: nothing installed behind the refusal

== what a missing sidecar does
   ok  no-sig: refused (exit 1) -- error: required signature sidecar unavailable for chaos-linux-x64
   ok  no-sig: nothing installed behind the refusal

== what a valid key that is not ours does
   ok  wrong-key: refused (exit 1) -- error: signature verification FAILED for chaos-linux-x64
   ok  wrong-key: nothing installed behind the refusal

== what a blank key does, and when
   ok  present-but-blank key is refused with the reason, not treated as unset
   ok  it refused before requesting anything: the fixture served zero files

== what an unusable manifest or a truncated transfer does
   ok  no-sums-entry: refused (exit 1) -- error: SHA256SUMS has no entry for chaos-linux-x64
   ok  no-sums-entry: nothing installed behind the refusal
   ok  html-sums: refused a manifest served as an HTML error page (exit 1)
   ok  html-sums: the guard that fired is the one that rejects proxy error pages, and nothing was installed
   ok  empty-artifact: refused (exit 1) -- error: download failed for https://github.com/chao2hang/chaos-code/releases/
   ok  empty-artifact: nothing installed behind the refusal

== what the two escape hatches actually cost
   ok  with the checksum skipped, the tampered artifact is still refused by the signature
   ok  skipping the signature alone still verifies the checksum
   note  both hatches together install a tampered artifact, which is what 'you are then trusting the download' means; no document in this repo sets either

== summary
   34 checks run against a release this lab built itself, in a container with no route to the network
   notes:
  - both hatches together install a tampered artifact, which is what 'you are then trusting the download' means; no document in this repo sets either
all 34 release-integrity checks passed
